.bd
DEVICE
.in +5
.br
.bd
kmem
- kernel memory device
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Char major 2, minor 1.
.bd
/dev/kmem
reads and writes the kernel's own 64K address space, the space task 0
runs in: the resident kernel, its windows, and the u page.  A seek
sets the address of a transfer, and a read or write advances it.

Only the CPU board's first page, 0x0000 to 0x0FFF, holds hardware.
A transfer that would touch it is answered as a whole from the zero
page, or discarded when it is a write, and the whole count is
reported with no error.  The front-panel task and mask registers and
the map registers are not there to be read or changed.  The 1K of RAM
at 0x0000 and the monitor EPROM at 0x0800 are memory, and a transfer
wholly inside either block is served.

A transfer that would run past 0xFFFF wraps at the top of the space
and is answered the same way, with zeroes.

A write changes the running kernel.  One to the wrong address crashes
the machine, and the device should be protected accordingly even though
ps only needs to read it.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
LOW MEMORY
.in +5
.br
A few things the kernel wants other programs to find are left at
fixed low addresses.  A program seeks to one and reads it; the
lengths of a table's entries still come from the headers the
program is built with, but the table's address and size need not
be.

.in +10
.ti -10
0x1000		The kernel's entry point, a jump instruction.
.ti -10
0x1003		The address of the process table, a word.
.bd
ps
seeks here and reads the pointer.
.ti -10
0x1005		The address of the mount table, a word.
.bd
df
seeks here and reads the pointer.
.ti -10
0x30a		The number of entries in the mount table, a byte.
The kernel writes it from its own NMOUNT at boot, so df and
kstat report the running kernel's size and are not rebuilt when
it changes.

.ti -10
0x30b		The number of buffer cache headers, a byte, written
when the cache reaches its final size.  A pool of exactly 256
reads as zero; the pool never has fewer than eight, so zero
means 256.

.sp
The words at 0x1003 and 0x1005 are low byte first.  The
addresses are the kernel's own; the kernel's symbol table in
.bd
/micronix
is the general way to find everything else, and is what kstat
reads.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
